-- title:
--   Diamond-twisted integrality implies membership in the two-cusp integral set
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and let $H$ be a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ which contains every unit whose image under the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$. Let $h$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for the group [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133), the subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$ obtained as the image under the inclusion of $\Gamma_0(M)$ of the preimage of $H$ under the homomorphism $\Gamma_0(M) \to (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ sending $\gamma$ to its lower-right entry mod $M$. Assume that for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, every Atkin–Lehner datum $W'$ for $(M,p)$ — that is, a factorisation $M = pR$ together with integers $a,b$ satisfying $pa - Rb = 1$ — and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion (taken with respect to the width $1$) of the diamond translate [`CuspForm.diamondLinH 2 d h`](def/CuspForm_HeckeOperatorFormsGammaH.html#L132) and the $n$-th such coefficient of its weight-$2$ slash by the matrix attached to $W'$ both lie in the bottom subring $\bot$ of $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. in the image of $\mathbb{Z}$. The conclusion is that $h$ lies in [`CuspForm.twoCuspIntegralSet M H 2 p ⊥`](def/CuspForm_TwoCuspLattice.html#L54): for every element $t$ of the subring `heckeRingH M H 2` of $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphisms of weight-$2$ cusp forms for [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) generated by the range of `heckeGenH` for the empty set of excluded primes, every Atkin–Lehner datum $W$ for $(M,p)$ and every $n$, the $n$-th $q$-expansion coefficients of $t\,h$ and of the weight-$2$ slash of $t\,h$ by $W$ lie in $\bot$.
--
--   This is the saturation step for the two-cusp integral structure at a prime exactly dividing the level: integrality of the Fourier coefficients at $\infty$ of all diamond translates of $h$, and of their Atkin–Lehner transforms, propagates to the whole Hecke ring. It feeds the construction of the Atkin–Lehner transport automorphism of the integral two-cusp lattice modulo $p$ and the associated vanishing and reduction statements.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspForm_mem_twoCuspIntegralSet_of_forall_qCoeff_diamondLinH_mem.lean

